@page "/projects/seeding"

<ProjectPage>

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        I built this at <strong>Klarr Utvecklingsbyrå</strong>, a development agency, for an
        education platform, and it was the subject of my thesis. Developers needed realistic
        made-up data to work with, and the automated tests needed to start from exactly the
        same data every time. Each person was solving that in their own way.
    </p>

    <p>
        A tool like this is called a seeding system: it fills a database with starting data.
        Mine creates that data on demand, as much or as little as a task needs, with the
        pieces of data linked to each other just as they are in the real platform, including
        the unusual cases that only turn up in real use. It can rebuild everything from
        scratch, or <strong>top up just one part</strong> without wiping what a developer is
        in the middle of.
    </p>

    <h2>Built for other people to use</h2>

    <p>
        A quick throwaway program is easy. A tool that other developers will actually use,
        add to, and not resent is harder, and that shaped the design.
    </p>

    <p>
        Adding a new kind of data means writing one small, separate piece, not editing one
        big file that everyone shares. How much data to create, and how it connects, is a
        setting rather than written into the code, because a quick demo and a test of how the
        platform copes under heavy use need very different data. And everything it creates is
        <strong>checked against the same rules as the real application</strong>, so it can't
        quietly produce records the app would reject.
    </p>

    <h2>Finding out what was needed</h2>

    <p>
        Before building it, I talked to the developers who would rely on it, who worked in
        different ways, and looked at what the automated tests needed and where the platform
        was heading. They wanted different things: <strong>small, fast data</strong> for
        day-to-day work, and <strong>large, realistic data</strong> for meaningful testing.
    </p>

    <p>
        Making it adjustable met both needs instead of compromising on either. Balancing those
        competing needs across teams was the part I found most valuable, and it's what my
        thesis examined.
    </p>

    <h2>Built in, written down, handed over</h2>

    <p>
        The automated tests use the data it creates, so they always start from the same data
        instead of whatever happened to be in the database.
    </p>

    <p>
        I wrote documentation for new team members and presented the design to the
        development team. That mattered as much as the code: a tool nobody understands gets
        worked around, and one that only its author can change becomes that person's job
        forever.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
